## Founding & History
The Roslyn Estate, where Roslyn 1823 is located, dates back to 1823, making it a nearly 200-year-old historic homestead in Tasmania's Coal River Valley. The heritage-listed Georgian building was constructed entirely of convict bricks, which were made and fired on-site. These bricks were also used in the construction of nearby historic Richmond. The estate was connected to early settlers George Weston Gunning and George Burn. Today, Roslyn 1823 operates as Tasmania's only permaculture vineyard and farm.

## People
Roslyn 1823 is a small, family-run vineyard and farm. The owner and vigneron, Andrew Palmer, has studied and applied permaculture and Natural Sequence Farming techniques for over a decade. The winemaker for Roslyn 1823 is Robbie Drew from Tea Tree, who brings 30 years of experience to the role. Visitors can spend around 90 minutes with the owner during a private tour.

## Vineyard & Site
Roslyn 1823 is a permaculture-based vineyard integrated into a 30-hectare farm in the Coal River Valley, South Eastern Tasmania. It is noted as Tasmania's only permaculture vineyard. The vineyard's viticultural approach is guided by the three ethics (earth care, people care, and fair share) and 12 principles of permaculture. The farming approach is environmentally sustainable, with a motto of "Beyond Organic". Practices include using organic compost and mulching, biological fertilisers, and employing chickens, ducks, sheep, and alpacas for pest and weed control. Irrigation is kept to a minimum to ensure flavours reflect the terroir. Grapes are hand-picked, with only the highest quality bunches selected for premium wines. The Coal River Valley's cool, dry climate contributes to rich, fruit-forward wines.

Varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Riesling, Chardonnay, and Pinot Gris.

## Cellar Door Experience
Roslyn 1823 offers private homestead and vineyard tours by pre-booking only. Bookings are essential to avoid disappointment, with typical tour times at 11 am and 2 pm. Visitors can spend approximately 90 minutes with the owner of the vineyard. The tour includes a visit to the vines to learn about permaculture vineyard setup and operation. Wine tasting takes place in one of Australia's oldest cellar doors, located within the historic Roslyn Estate homestead. Guests can relax in original rooms such as the parlour, music room, cobblestoned stables, kitchen, and wood-fired oven. A selection of wines is included in the tour price, with three bottles of the visitor's choice opened during private tours.

The cellar door is open Friday, Saturday, and Sunday from 12 pm to 5 pm for general visits.

## Food & Hospitality
Roslyn 1823 offers special lunch tours on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, which include a tasty lunch and wines. During these tours, guests arrive at 11:30 am and spend two hours at the vineyard and homestead. Staff on-site can also provide information about special lunch and sunset dinner options. The facilities include an outdoor dining area and are family-friendly.

## Awards & Recognition
Roslyn 1823 produces award-winning Tasmanian Pinot Noir. Their Chablis style Chardonnay is also double award-winning.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Roslyn 1823 is Tasmania's only permaculture vineyard. The vineyard and farm are managed according to permaculture principles, aiming to work in harmony with nature to promote biodiversity and regenerate the earth. The viticultural approach is guided by permaculture ethics and principles, focusing on sustainability and environmental friendliness. They use organic compost and mulching, biological fertilisers, and natural pest and weed control methods involving chickens, ducks, sheep, and alpacas. Irrigation is kept to a minimum. Their motto is "Beyond Organic". The wines are described as sustainably produced.

## Visiting Practical Info
**Address:** 409 White Kangaroo Road, Campania, Tasmania, 7026, Australia.
**Phone:** 0418 713 566 (available weekdays from 10 am - 3 pm).
**Email:** wine@roslyn1823.com.au.
**Website:** http://roslyn-1823.com/.
**Social Media:** Facebook, Instagram.
**Opening Hours (Cellar Door):** Friday, Saturday, Sunday: 12 pm – 5 pm.
**Tours:** Private Homestead and Vineyard Tours are by pre-booking only, generally at 11 am and 2 pm.
**Facilities:** Carpark, Cellar Door, Coach Parking, Family Friendly, Lawn/Gardens, Online Orders, Pet Friendly (enquire), Wine Tasting, Free Wifi, Disabled access available (contact operator for details).

## Notable / Distinctive
Roslyn 1823 is distinctive as Tasmania's only permaculture vineyard. The estate itself, dating back to 1823, is a nearly 200-year-old historic homestead and one of Tasmania's oldest heritage-listed estates. Its cellar door is considered one of Australia's oldest. The Georgian-style building was constructed entirely from convict bricks, made and fired on-site, and features a unique imposing tower. The property has original features such as cobblestoned stables, a kitchen, a smoke room, a parlour, and a music room. The owner, Andrew Palmer, has a teaching degree and planned and set up the vineyard based on permaculture design principles and ethics.

